1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,175
Most organizations can tell you what they spend in the cloud.

2
00:00:10,225 --> 00:00:17,300
Far fewer can show who is accountable for reducing it, and how much of that

3
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:18,725
reduction is real.

4
00:00:18,775 --> 00:00:22,050
That is what FinOps Command Center does.

5
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:27,750
This is a local demonstration on illustrative data, with a simulated sign-in.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,068
This is the leadership view.

7
00:00:36,118 --> 00:00:41,056
We run at roughly $12.2 million a month.

8
00:00:41,106 --> 00:00:49,785
There is $19.3 million a year in open savings opportunity, and $2.76 million already

9
00:00:49,835 --> 00:00:50,409
verified.

10
00:00:50,459 --> 00:00:52,279
Verified, not estimated.

11
00:00:52,329 --> 00:00:59,138
But only 57% of that open opportunity has an accountable owner.

12
00:00:59,188 --> 00:01:04,750
More than $8 million a year belongs to nobody.

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,036
Leadership can drill from product, to owner, to the individual recommendation, with

14
00:01:12,086 --> 00:01:16,169
estimated and verified savings always shown separately.

15
00:01:16,219 --> 00:01:20,893
In SAP, the largest unowned item is REC-2041:

16
00:01:20,943 --> 00:01:29,750
one point oh $8 million a year, validated, with 6 days left on its SLA.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,801
FinOps now takes ownership of the pipeline.

18
00:01:44,851 --> 00:01:48,959
FinOps routes and tracks the work;

19
00:01:49,009 --> 00:01:53,117
it does not approve technical changes.

20
00:01:53,167 --> 00:02:00,048
REC-2041 goes to Priya Raman, who owns the SAP platform.

21
00:02:00,098 --> 00:02:09,750
That starts the SLA clock, and the decision is recorded in the audit trail.

22
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,036
Now Priya signs in.

23
00:02:23,086 --> 00:02:34,607
Her queue holds only the work assigned to her, with this item at the top.

24
00:02:34,657 --> 00:02:43,093
She accepts it and opens a ticket, linked to the record.

25
00:02:43,143 --> 00:02:46,950
She submits a remediation plan:

26
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:55,436
a rolling resize in the weekend window, with a rollback path.

27
00:02:55,486 --> 00:03:05,464
Change approval stays with her change process, so she records the approval reference

28
00:03:05,514 --> 00:03:06,236
here;

29
00:03:06,286 --> 00:03:11,636
in this demonstration that step is simulated.

30
00:03:11,686 --> 00:03:22,436
Once the change is made, she submits implementation evidence, and the item moves to

31
00:03:22,486 --> 00:03:24,750
FinOps for verification.

32
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,265
FinOps signs back in and verifies the savings.

33
00:03:39,315 --> 00:03:45,198
In production that check runs against billing data after the change;

34
00:03:45,248 --> 00:03:47,356
here it is simulated.

35
00:03:47,406 --> 00:03:52,750
Only now does an estimate count as a realized saving.

36
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,676
Back in the leadership view, verified savings have moved from $2.76 million to $3.85

37
00:04:11,726 --> 00:04:15,415
million a year, within this demonstration.

38
00:04:15,465 --> 00:04:24,141
Leadership can trace that number to the product, to the accountable owner, and to

39
00:04:24,191 --> 00:04:29,750
the record itself, with its ticket and its evidence.

40
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,164
That is the process:

41
00:04:32,214 --> 00:04:37,145
find the highest-value opportunities, assign clear accountability, let engineering

42
00:04:37,195 --> 00:04:44,341
deliver through its own change process, and count savings only once they are

43
00:04:44,391 --> 00:04:44,894
verified.

44
00:04:44,944 --> 00:04:47,662
The figures today are illustrative;

45
00:04:47,712 --> 00:04:53,750
the operating model is what we would take into a pilot.
